WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.690
It is great to welcome back to the program
Congressman Roe Connor, who represents California's

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:09.070
17th District, located in the heart of Silicon
Valley.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:11.110
Great to have you back, Congressman.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.110
Appreciate it.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.019
David, I appreciate your show.

00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:19.030
I appreciated your show during the burning
campaign and look forward to coming on as

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:20.640
often as you'll have me.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.640
Yes.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:22.730
Well, we will talk about the Bernie campaign.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.170
But in terms of where we are now, we're it
looks like Joe Biden will.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:33.410
He said he will announce his V.P. pick around
August 1st is the latest that I've seen.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.810
You like Elizabeth Warren as Joe Biden's possible
V.P..

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:38.810
Why?

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:39.810
I do.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:44.710
I mean, I obviously I thought Bernie Sanders
would have been the ideal person to lead us

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:45.710
at this time.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.550
I was a co-chair of his campaign.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:51.489
And the reason is that Bernie Sanders understood
the importance of movement politics.

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:57.450
He understood that you don't achieve anything
just having smart people in Washington and

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.450
good policy.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.969
You need to have a mobilized base.

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:07.090
And I saw the passion that he inspired, particularly
among young people that said he's not going

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:08.450
to be the nominee.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:13.380
And obviously, he's not going to be in consideration
for Vice President Joe Biden said he wants

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.200
to have a woman on the ticket.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.819
And in that circumstance, it's obvious to
me that the most progressive possibility is

00:01:19.819 --> 00:01:21.389
Elizabeth Warren.

00:01:21.389 --> 00:01:22.759
She's also exceedingly competent.

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:27.389
I mean, when I have worked with her, she really
understands federal agencies.

00:01:27.389 --> 00:01:31.659
She really understands how the Senate works,
how the House works.

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:37.399
And in fact, I had recommended when it looked
like Bernie Sanders was going to be possibly

00:01:37.399 --> 00:01:44.029
the nominee, that he pick Elizabeth Warren,
given her expertize and knowledge of the federal

00:01:44.029 --> 00:01:48.030
government and her commitment to progressive
ideals.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:51.569
So I think for the same reason, she would
be very good for Vice President Biden and

00:01:51.569 --> 00:01:52.859
Morse.

00:01:52.859 --> 00:01:59.469
Urgent, given we need a true progressive in
that there is often these conventional wisdoms,

00:01:59.469 --> 00:02:03.909
which very often or not are not really that
wise about.

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:09.129
You know, you need to think about what part
of the country you're V.P. is from in terms

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:14.640
of appealing maybe to Southerners or Midwesterners
or, you know, having a strategy that appeals

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.190
more broadly than a northeastern Democrat.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:23.280
However, the latest polling I've seen suggests
that Elizabeth Warren may actually be the

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.939
option that gives Joe Biden the largest polling
boost.

00:02:26.939 --> 00:02:32.390
Is this something unique about Warren in your
mind, or is the the dynamic this time around

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.950
just so different that those conventional
wisdoms maybe don't apply?

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.849
You know, it's I've never been a big fan of
that conventional wisdom thinking, and it's

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:43.290
convenient now, it doesn't apply when you
have a Bill Clinton and Al Gore, when you

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.370
have sort of moderate tickets, it seems OK.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:46.370
OK.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:47.469
Let's double down.

00:02:47.469 --> 00:02:52.359
My view is that the vice presidential pick
should be someone who people will see as a

00:02:52.359 --> 00:02:54.420
governing partner.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.159
Even though I totally disagreed with them,
I thought Bush's pick of Dick Cheney was very

00:02:58.159 --> 00:03:00.019
strategically smart at the time.

00:03:00.019 --> 00:03:02.870
People were questioning his competence to
lead on foreign policy.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:07.939
And he was sort of reassuring people saying,
look, I've got this person, Cheney.

00:03:07.939 --> 00:03:12.840
And so in this case, I think people are going
to ask the question, who is going to be able

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.579
to help rebuild the economy?

00:03:14.579 --> 00:03:17.650
Who is going to really understand competence
in government?

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:21.930
And under those metrics, I think Elizabeth
Warren really stands out.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.090
And I think that's one of the reasons you're
seeing the polling.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:29.280
The second reason you're seeing the polling
is a lot of younger voters and voters of color

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.349
actually support her policies that are for
a green new deal, for Medicare for all.

00:03:34.349 --> 00:03:38.840
Therefore, free public college, they're most
affected by student loans.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:45.040
So you're seeing that she has a lot of support
and across the board with Democrats, but also

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.610
young voters.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:52.609
And finally, and this is no detraction from
other candidates.

00:03:52.609 --> 00:03:55.470
The fact is that she has a tight, trusted
brand.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:56.470
Right.

00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:00.319
So, I mean, if you were to poll Elizabeth
worth it more and me, you know, even though

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:05.060
I have all the policies, Warren would be overwhelmingly
liked because people trust her.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:06.159
They know her.

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:09.780
And I think at a time of crisis, that matters.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:14.639
And so just the fact of her familiarity, while
I may not be an advantage in a different election

00:04:14.639 --> 00:04:18.900
year, I think in this election year, really
a place to her benefit.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.539
There is a contingent of those who supported
Bernie Sanders.

00:04:22.539 --> 00:04:24.340
I don't know what the size of it is.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:25.340
I know.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:28.539
If you spend a lot of time online, it feels
bigger.

00:04:28.539 --> 00:04:33.970
If you spend more time, you know, in Ohio
knocking, it probably feels smaller of people

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:40.270
that supported Bernie that are not looking
like they are interested in voting for Joe

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.270
Biden.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:43.610
And I've addressed this before.

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.750
I'm not planning to shame anybody for their
vote.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:52.740
If someone genuinely feels that they'd rather
four more years of Trump than Biden, by all

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.290
means, they should act in accordance with
that.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:05:04.210
But for those who are looking to either punish
the DNC or send a message or any of these

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:08.979
other things, do you have a message to that
contingent of those who were with you during

00:05:08.979 --> 00:05:10.509
the primary with Bernie?

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:11.509
Sure.

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:13.070
I'd say do it in other ways.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:18.961
I mean, if you want to run progressive challengers
to people in the House or the Senate, even

00:05:18.961 --> 00:05:25.060
if I can't support it, I appreciate that and
understand that if you want to have a challenge

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:29.220
to the DNC, I appreciate that and support
that.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:35.860
If you want to make sure that in in in twenty,
twenty four we have a progressive nominee,

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:40.449
even if, you know, it's getting behind whoever,
even if it's not the vice president.

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:41.449
Fine.

00:05:41.449 --> 00:05:42.990
I support that.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:50.340
But don't sabotage the progressive movement
by having four more years of Trump in.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:52.750
And that's the reason that's a sabotages,
mostly.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.099
Partly because of the courts.

00:05:55.099 --> 00:06:00.330
I mean, let's assume Bernie Sanders, who knows
if he runs again, let's say he became president

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:01.330
in 2024.

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:06.729
You know, I mean, in four years, Alder's,
early 80s and we had eight years of Trump.

00:06:06.729 --> 00:06:10.909
I mean, what could Bernie Sanders do in a
Supreme Court that was seven to two against

00:06:10.909 --> 00:06:13.410
us in agencies that have been totally.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:14.410
Got it.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:19.800
He would basically basically be a bridge president,
then trying to fix the Supreme Court and trying

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.060
to undo the damage.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:26.740
And it would take another 10 years before
the we had progressive policy.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:30.840
On the other hand, let's say we had Joe Biden
and then in twenty four we had Bernie Sanders

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.670
or a progressive.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:36.530
You know that then you're at least having
more forward progress.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:39.449
So I'm for a pragmatic about it, but I agree.

00:06:39.449 --> 00:06:45.000
Ultimately, I think the vote is sacred and
we have to earn people's vote not to sort

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.629
of badger them into supporting the ticket.

00:06:48.629 --> 00:06:54.199
And so you it sounds like you don't subscribe
to the more acceleration is the ideas of sometimes

00:06:54.199 --> 00:07:00.629
you've got to burn it down to build it back
up or Trump being who Trump is, will accelerate

00:07:00.629 --> 00:07:03.159
some much more progressive leftward swing.

00:07:03.159 --> 00:07:07.470
I mean, I guess it didn't seem to happen over
the last four years in the sense that Joe

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.210
Biden and ended up the nominee ultimately.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:11.639
But that doesn't sound like a philosophy you
subscribe to.

00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:16.759
I guess I guess it's a philosophy of a bit
of privilege, right?

00:07:16.759 --> 00:07:20.810
I mean, even if it were true and who knows
if it were if it were true that there is going

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.660
to be some opposite reaction.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:31.909
Are we willing to sacrifice the kids in cages
in the meantime, or are we willing to sacrifice

00:07:31.909 --> 00:07:35.000
the Muslim American Muslims who are not allowed
into this country?

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.590
Are we willing to sacrifice?

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:40.130
The environmental damage that's happening
every day.

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:42.860
Are we willing to sacrifice the court?

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:50.080
So it seems to me not consistent with progressive
ideals of every life matters and that the

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:51.780
ends don't justify the means.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:55.449
I also don't think it's necessarily true.

00:07:55.449 --> 00:08:01.830
I mean, I think that Trump will do so much
damage in stoking racial division and stoking

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:08.150
xenophobia that if he has eight years left
at the next president, that's probably going

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:14.639
to have to do a lot of cleanup and isn't going
to be in a position as easily of pushing bold,

00:08:14.639 --> 00:08:15.669
progressive ideas.

00:08:15.669 --> 00:08:21.750
Whereas if you have Biden, the next president
after him could do that.

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:22.750
We essentially agree.

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:27.050
So let's let's move on to to another topic
that I want to talk about, which is the sort

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:31.160
of next six to 12 months related to Corona
virus.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:37.040
I think one of the worst things that could
happen would be sort of overlapping openings

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:43.310
and closures meant to try to try to play whack
a mole while we still have no effective treatment

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:46.550
and we still wait for for a vaccine.

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:52.250
What are you sort of thinking is the most
likely trajectory over the next six months,

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:57.740
given what we're seeing at the state level
right now and federal leadership on the virus?

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:59.580
Well, I appreciate your saying that.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.940
I mean, first we have to look at what could
have been not you look at countries like South

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:04.940
Korea.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:09.350
Now, South Korea did things about compromising
people's privacy, which we would never do

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:10.350
here.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:11.350
But they get some very simple things.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:14.020
They told everyone to wear masks right in
the beginning.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:17.070
Our CDC was going back and forth on that.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:21.520
They had us testing out there and they work
even with private sector testing companies.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:27.510
Our government was arrogant to reject not
only W World Health Organization's tests,

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:31.780
but also to reject the private sector's help
in testing.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:34.620
So and we should have had a lockdown much
earlier.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:37.960
I called for a national lockdown in March.

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:43.540
And if we had done that for a couple of weeks,
we would have avoided some of the the the

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:46.070
the deaths, even New York.

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:52.670
And I don't want to cast blame on any particular
leaders, but if they had locked down when

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:58.310
Santa Clara County did, the former CDC head
estimates that, you know, almost 80 percent

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:00.880
of deaths could have been avoided.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:05.400
All of this is to say that these should be
lessons going forward.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:16.230
And my view going forward is that we ought
to have a clear national guidelines on where

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:21.570
we need for masks, where we need for social
distancing, where we need policies in terms

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:25.660
of non gathered, not gathering and in large
areas.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:32.690
I understand locally people can then determine
whether whether to start some business or

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:33.690
not.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:37.840
But I think there has to be some national
guidelines as well.

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:42.550
And then we need a greater focus on on testing
and a greater focus on the adoption.

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:45.090
You know, Google, Apple, it is my district.

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:46.490
They've come up with this app.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:52.310
It would be voluntary in terms of people display
disclosing their information and the information

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:53.310
would be private.

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:55.410
But I think we need to try to get them more
adopted.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.350
I think that could help.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:11:00.930
I want to talk about testing in a moment,
but let's talk about the earlier lockdown

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:02.520
briefly.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:05.760
What was the date that Santa Clara lockdown,
do you remember offhand?

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.700
March, March 16th, 16th.

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:09.430
Okay, so that's a little.

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:13.820
I've said that somewhere between March 5th
and 15th.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:16.030
We had nearly 100 hundred cases in China.

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:20.880
Italy already was around four to six thousand
and we had several hundred cases.

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:24.520
That to me, seems like it would have been
enough information for Donald Trump to say

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:27.730
this is going to be tough, but I need all
50 governors with me.

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:29.600
We need 30 days of stay at home.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:32.760
It probably would have had to be extended
another 30.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:34.510
It would have ended on May 5th.

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:39.130
Like that 60 day period could have been over
by May 5th or May 10th or May 15th.

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:43.440
And when I mentioned this to Joe Rogan last
week, he said there was no app.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:47.610
You wouldn't have gotten anybody to do it
on March 5th or 10th or 15th because there

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:49.570
was no appetite for it.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:53.720
Doesn't that miss that 40 percent of the country
will essentially agree with whatever Donald

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:58.340
Trump proposes and maybe it actually could
have been done at that earlier stage?

00:11:58.340 --> 00:11:59.420
It actually could have been done.

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:04.770
I mean, Dr. Sarah Cody, who did this on March
30th, she had the benefit of being married

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:11.070
to an epidemiologist at Stanford who told
her that every single day that you wait, you're

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:13.230
actually creating exponential risk.

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:17.060
And that's why she convinced the six payor
counties to shelter in place in Santa Clara

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:21.260
County, which was one of the earliest hit,
has only had about one hundred and twenty

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:24.750
deaths as a result of hard her decision.

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:27.740
So and they complied very strongly.

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:29.340
People in my district have complied.

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:32.720
I believe Trump had done that.

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:33.720
People would have complied.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:35.790
I have no doubt about it.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:39.380
And my guess is he probably would have made
that decision in retrospect, because it would

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:46.020
have put him in a better position for reelection
if he could have done that in March and come

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:49.000
April, in mid April or May.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.980
We had avoided the worst of it and the economy
was reopening.

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:57.370
I think he underestimated how severe this
was going to be, how long it was going to

00:12:57.370 --> 00:12:58.370
last.

00:12:58.370 --> 00:13:02.730
And, you know, it's a little bit of Monday
morning quarterbacking, but I think when it

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:07.870
really shows is the need not just for us to
listen to science, that's everyone says that

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:09.270
and that's the easy part.

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:15.440
But how do you create a culture where we communicate
science in a way that speaks to people's lived

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:17.200
experience and isn't rejected?

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:22.300
And what is that dialog and communication
and how do we facilitate that?

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:26.850
And I and I think we have large questions
in our democracy to understand that because

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:31.360
climate change, so many of these issues are
going to raise similar questions where your

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:34.960
intuition is contradicting scientific information.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:37.690
And how do we act in that in those cases?

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:41.800
This is one of the things I talked about earlier
this week, that scary, which is that what

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:49.900
is really a medical situation has become a
proxy culture war where it's about tyranny

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:51.060
and liberty.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:54.310
It's about the mask as a political signal.

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:57.780
And it's completely divorced from from the
scientific reality.

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:02.280
And I understand like tomorrow on the show,
I'm going to do a hypothetical, what would

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:07.810
be a good reason for a left wing skepticism
of a total lockdown?

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:11.450
And it's going to be a sort of very careful
examination of different elements of this.

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:17.470
But the way in which this has become a culture
war that is divorced from science is seems

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:19.920
acutely disturbing.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:20.920
I agree with that.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:24.860
And I think we on the left have to be careful,
too, because I think when we start the conversation

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:30.180
with people on the right don't believe in
science, I think it puts people in a defensive

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:31.180
posture.

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:32.180
Right.

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:33.180
And I think that there's probably.

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:34.350
Here's what I think is actually going on.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:39.140
I mean, this is just a bit speculation, but
my sense is that people just have different

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:46.970
probabilities of risk and different tolerance
for death and different tolerance for economic

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:47.970
decline.

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:51.090
And what you're really seeing is a conflict
of those values.

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:55.310
So I don't think people on on the right are
out there saying, well, we don't believe that

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:59.220
a small minority, but the vast majority are
not saying we don't believe call it exists

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:02.130
or is being transmitted or that masks how.

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:05.880
But they're saying, you know, the probability
is so small and get on with it.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:07.200
And we're rugged and tough.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:08.790
And this is what we're going to do.

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:14.480
It's a question as a democracy is how do we
have the conversation to be more about the

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:22.510
clash of values, which is really where it
is, and a given take rather than the politicization

00:15:22.510 --> 00:15:23.610
of science.

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:30.060
And I think what you're seeing is the politicization
of science is really about a clash of values.

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:35.120
So people are trying to politicize science
to have their value that the economics matter

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:38.820
more than saving certain lives.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:44.080
Last thing I want to touch on in terms of
testing, what is the number of daily tests

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:46.710
that you believe is where we should be?

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:48.510
It what from what I'm seeing?

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:52.280
If we don't get to five hundred thousand a
day, there's not really even very much to

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:53.280
talk about.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:54.280
But that.

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:55.280
Right.

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:58.210
It's got to be somewhere between one and a
half and five million tests a day.

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:03.180
Where do you have a sense that that's what
I've heard from Fouchier from from the CDC

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:07.790
that, you know, I've heard ideally it would
be a million tests a day, but short of that,

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:09.680
around four to five million a week.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:11.490
I think that would be important.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:12.890
But it's not just the testing.

00:16:12.890 --> 00:16:17.070
It's then what do you do with the testing
and what are you doing in terms of contact

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:18.070
tracing?

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:22.220
What are you doing in terms of isolating these
individuals?

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:26.500
What are you doing in terms of testing the
right people that they're testing folks who

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:30.270
are in packed circumstances or central workers?

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:31.270
So.

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:33.390
Yes, we need more capacity for testing.

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:36.590
But when you look at a country like South
Korea and they didn't do everything right,

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:38.630
they had violations of civil liberties.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:44.180
But because of their Saar's experience, they
had a lot of cultural norms about what they

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:47.870
will do with the testing and who did test
and how to contact Crace.

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:52.050
And hopefully our country will develop some
of that having gone through this experience.

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:57.620
Yeah, certainly it's not just the testing,
but the testing is it's it's necessary, but

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:01.640
not sufficient, I guess is the point that
you're that you're and that's just that's

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:04.500
a succinct way to say what I was trying to
say.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:09.399
We've been speaking with Congressman Roe Cano,
who represents California's 17th District.

00:17:09.399 --> 00:17:10.970
Congressman, always great having you on.

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:11.970
I appreciate it.

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:13.219
Thanks for having me.

